# a9-gpu.config -- added by build.sh ONLY with A9_ANDROID=1 A9_GPU=1 (runs/gpu.md,
# review 27 item 1). A bounded, in-kernel end for the hangs a first secure-world
# GPU activation can cause, independent of userspace: a CPU stuck in an SMC
# (interrupts never reach it) is caught by the buddy hard-lockup detector on
# another CPU; a task waiting forever in an uninterruptible SCM completion is
# caught by the hung-task detector. Both panic; panic=10 (cmdline) reboots and
# the PON beacon 2 sends the reboot to bootloader fastboot, where the host
# supervisor (gpu/g1-supervise.sh) restores slot a. Whether PSCI reset itself
# completes while the secure world is wedged is NOT known (hardware).
CONFIG_SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR=y
CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_SOFTLOCKUP_PANIC=1
CONFIG_HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR=y
CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_HARDLOCKUP_PANIC=y
CONFIG_DETECT_HUNG_TASK=y
CONFIG_DEFAULT_HUNG_TASK_TIMEOUT=120
CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_HUNG_TASK_PANIC=1
# G1 attempt 1 (2026-10-16, MEASURED): with gpucc as a module the Adreno SMMU
# (59a0000.iommu, arm-smmu built in) hit the deferred-probe timeout at 11.7 s
# before any vendor module could load ("probe with driver arm-smmu failed with
# error -110", permanent), and adreno then probed without it and failed. The
# GPU clock controller is therefore built in, so its consumers that are built
# in (the SMMU) bind at boot; gpu/check-probe-order.py enforces the rule.
CONFIG_SM_GPUCC_6115=y
